    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 272233\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n333 PM PDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Dangerous heat expected across the Mojave Desert through Friday. \n\n* Chances of showers and thunderstorms across the region today \n  through Saturday, with gusty winds and heavy rain being the \n  greatest threat.\n\n* Gusty afternoon winds possible Saturday and Sunday near 40 mph at \n  times. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Early Next Week\n\nWell above normal temperatures continue through the rest of the week \nacross the Mojave Desert as strong 500 mb ridging is centered over \nthe Four Corners region. Upper air observations indicate 597dm \nheights under the ridge, which is well above average for this time \nof year. In addition well above normal low and mid level temperature \nanomalies support extreme heat persisting through at least tomorrow. \nRecord highs were either broken or tied at Las Vegas, Kingman, and \nDesert Rock on Wednesday, and it looks like another day of near-\nrecord heat before slight cooling on Friday-though temperatures will \nstill remain several degrees above normal. Today is expected to be \nthe hottest day of the week with most areas seeing readings about 8-\n10 degrees above normal. An approaching trough over the eastern \nPacific will help to suppress the ridge and shift it eastward Friday \nthrough the weekend, resulting in lowering heights and slightly \ncooler temperatures. Despite a slow downward trend in temperatures \ninto the weekend, they will still remain several degrees above \nnormal for late summer. An Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect \nthrough Friday for the Mojave Desert. While afternoon high \ntemperatures have been this hot (or hotter) earlier in the summer \nwithout heat headlines, it is important to note that there is \nhistorically a resurgence of heat-related impacts at the end of the \nseason. See the CLIMATE section at the bottom for daily records. \n\nDespite the presence of extreme heat, monsoon season is still here \nwith chances for showers and storms today through Saturday for \nportions of NW Arizona, the Colorado River Valley, and Southern \nNevada, and Northern Inyo County. Deepest moisture today remains \nover the lower Colorado River Valley and within Mohave County where \nPW remains near 1.1\" but surface dew points are only in the 40s to \nlow 50s. KLAS ACARs and RAOB soundings do show deeper upper level \nmoisture today than yesterday at this time, so would not be \nsurprised if convection today and tomorrow are a bit higher in \ncoverage than the previous few days. Satellite imagery this \nafternoon does show agitated Cu across the Mojave Desert and \nisolated storms have already been popping up. An increase in \ncoverage through 00-03z is expected before loss of instability after \nsunset allows convection to wane. Any storm does have the potential \nto produce locally heavy rainfall rates, wind gusts to 60 mph, and \nlightning. Deeper moisture surges northward tonight through Saturday \nwith dew points in the 50s and 60s and PW in the 1.00 to 1.40 inch \nrange. An approaching trough will also bring stronger upper level \nwinds from 700mb up to 300mb, resulting in better shear profiles for \nconvective organization. While the overall daily storm coverage can \neasily be impacted by leftover clouds from the previous days \nconvection, confidence is increasing better storm coverage Friday \nand Saturday for much of the region. Mid range guidance does show \ndrier air advecting into the region Sunday into early next week \nlikely keeping monsoon activity at bay or confined solely to \nMohave County for a few days. \n\nAlso watching Northern Inyo County as a stronger jet streak \nassociated with the approaching trough from the west is advecting in \ndeeper tropical moisture from the pacific. While an increase in \nclouds is limiting overall instability, steeper mid and upper lapse \nrates may be enough to support locally higher rainfall rates at \ntimes in and around the Rock Fire burn scar. Satellite and radar \nimagery this afternoon shows largely stratiform rain moving into the \nSierra and Inyo County, so will keep a close eye on this area in the \nevent flooding does become a concern. Less concerned with rainfall \nrates high enough to produce flash flooding today. Due to a stronger \njet influence aloft, light to moderate rain will likely continue \ninto tonight for Inyo county as this is not diurnally driven. \nTomorrow appears to have a better chance at afternoon convection \nnear Inyo county where brief periods of higher rainfall rates are \npossible. \n\nWindy conditions possible this weekend from in the southern portion \nof the forecast area including San Bernardino, Clark, and Mohave \nCounties. A stronger mid level jet streak of 35-45 kts moves \noverhead this weekend ahead of the incoming trough. Strong afternoon \nmixing into this jet streak may produce surface wind gusts over 40 \nmph at times. Best day for strongest winds appears to be Sunday as \nstronger vort max associated with the upper trough axis pushes \nthrough. Keeping winds under advisory criteria at the moment, but if \nforecast trends continue a Wind Advisory may be needed for portions \nof the area this weekend. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package... \nSoutherly winds with gusts around 20kts are building with some \nspotty showers developing in the valley. Chances for storms will be \npossible through the mid evening, the main threats being lightning \nand gusty, erratic winds to around 30kts. Storm activity leaves in \nthe mid evening and winds reduce for the night. Temperatures look \ncool below 100 shortly before 04z. Winds weaken into Friday morning, \nbecoming light and variable. Gusty south-southwest winds arrive \nFriday afternoon with some passing showers and storms. Friday's \nstorm chances look to erode in the early evening as winds reduce.\n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...00Z Forecast Package... Breezy and gusty southerly \nwinds continue with developing chances for scattered to isolated \nstorms and showers. Storms will offer lightning and locally strong \nand erratic gusts. Expect storm and shower chances for all but KBIH \nto exit into the mid evening to early night. KBIH keeps off and on \nshower chances through the night with chances increasing again into \nthe late night through the mid morning followed by more storm \nchances into Friday afternoon. Winds are expected to reduce at all \nsites into the evening and night, but gusts from distant storms may \nbriefly affect wind directions. Additional southerly winds and storm \nchances arrive into Friday afternoon across the region, but the best \nchances look to stay with KBIH.\n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE...Numerous climate locations have a forecast high\ntemperature and/or forecast warm low temperature within 3 degrees of \nthe daily record.\n\nThe table below shows the daily record maximum temperature and the \nyear the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which records \nare in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nMAX           THU, AUG 27 FRI, AUG 28 \n              Record (Yr) Record(Yr) \n\nLas Vegas     110(2017)*  110(2019)*\nBishop        103(2019)*  103(2017)\nNeedles       120(1891)   116(2023)*\nDaggett       111(2017)   111(2017)\nKingman       107(2017)*  107(2009)\nDesert Rock   107(2017)   107(2017)\nDeath Valley  124(1924)   120(2011)*\n\nThe table below shows the daily record warm minimum temperature and \nthe year the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which \nrecords are in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nWARM MIN      THU, AUG 27 FRI, AUG 28\n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     87(2011)*   86(2019)* \nBishop        67(1970)    62(1970) \nNeedles       93(2011)    94(2017) \nDaggett       81(2011)*   82(2017)* \nKingman       79(2020)*   75(2019)* \nDesert Rock   78(1995)*   77(2011)* \nDeath Valley  95(2011)*  100(2019)* \n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Moore\nAVIATION...Murdock\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
